fpcmoh: do not advance an SSM that has just been marked failed

Both receive callbacks did this:

    if (ret)
      fpi_ssm_mark_failed (transfer->ssm, error);
    fpi_ssm_next_state (transfer->ssm);

fpi_ssm_mark_failed() sets machine->completed (or moves it to the cleanup
state). Calling fpi_ssm_next_state() afterwards trips the guard in
fpi-ssm.c:

    BUG: (machine->completed): fpi_ssm_next_state()

which aborts the daemon instead of failing the operation. On 10a5:a921 the
init state fails routinely -- the sensor answers CMD_INIT with an event this
driver does not parse yet -- so every attempt killed fprintd. Return after
marking the machine failed; that path is terminal.

Also apply the same rule to fpi_ssm_hello_receive_cb's -1 branch, which has
the identical shape and would assert the same way if a hello buffer ever
failed to parse.

No behaviour change on the success path.

--- a/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
@@ -1957,6 +1957,7 @@
     {
       fpi_ssm_mark_failed (transfer->ssm,
                            fpi_device_error_new (FP_DEVICE_ERROR_GENERAL));
+      return;
     }
 
   fpi_ssm_next_state (transfer->ssm);
@@ -2009,8 +2010,11 @@
   int ret = fpc_init_evt_handler (device, transfer->buffer, transfer->actual_length);
 
   if (ret)
-    fpi_ssm_mark_failed (transfer->ssm,
-                         fpi_device_error_new (FP_DEVICE_ERROR_GENERAL));
+    {
+      fpi_ssm_mark_failed (transfer->ssm,
+                           fpi_device_error_new (FP_DEVICE_ERROR_GENERAL));
+      return;
+    }
 
   fpi_ssm_next_state (transfer->ssm);
 }
